module std.nat


// A natural number is a CARRIER, not an operations table. The prior declaration
// -- Nat = CommutativeSemiring<std.magnitude.Magnitude> -- said a natural number IS a record of
// {add, zero, mul, one}, naming the STRUCTURE where the carrier belonged. Its own
// module contradicted it: nat_compare and nat_max order two Nats with `<` and `>`,
// which no operations-record admits. The import era never made a consumer check
// the declaration, so the contradiction sat unobserved rather than being allowed.
//
// The semiring is a VALUE the carrier inhabits (v2.std.nat models it that way, and
// DESIGN §4 states the rule: operations come from inhabitance, as Int.add derives
// from Int inhabiting a ring). No such value is declared here because nothing in
// the corpus consumes a Nat's .add/.zero/.mul/.one -- adding one now would be
// speculative structure, not grounding.
//
// REPAIR 2026-08-17 (second attempt, different layer). The FIRST attempt was
// `Int where range(min: 0)`, which is NOT AVAILABLE here because std.integer declares
//     type Int = AbelianGroup<GroupCompletion<std.nat.Nat>>
// so a Nat defined over Int closes the cycle Nat -> Int -> GroupCompletion<Nat>.
// A whole-tree compile at 3e493652f7a aborted with `has overflowed its stack`
// inside reconcile, where the immediately preceding commit reached reconcile and
// completed it. Whether the bare `Int` here reaches the kernel primitive (as
// std.types' EpochSecs and five siblings do, compiling fine) or std.integer's
// algebraic Int is exactly the pool-dependent binding this branch is about --
// which is why that position could not take the idiom on faith.
//
// The carrier below routes through NOTHING, so no cycle is reachable from it. It is
// also the shape v2 already uses -- src/v2/std/nat.dag declares `type Nat` opaque --
// so this makes the two declarations agree instead of forking (DESIGN section 3), and
// it drops the operations-record the header above shows the module contradicting.
type Nat

fn nat_compare(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> std.algebra.Ordering {
  if a < b { Less } else { if a > b { Greater } else { Equal } }
}

fn nat_max(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Nat {
  if a > b { a } else { b }
}

fn nat_min(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Nat {
  if a < b { a } else { b }
}

// AN ASCENDING RUN OF NATURALS IS ONE CONCEPT AND THIS IS ITS ONLY AUTHORITY. It lives beside Nat
// rather than beside any consumer because the run is a fact about the naturals, not about runner
// slots or DRAM channels; two consumers had each open-coded it as a bucket over hardcoded literal
// lists, and both buckets were stranded at the widths their author happened to enumerate.
//
// AN EMPTY RUN IS AN ANSWER, NOT AN ABSENCE. lo > hi means the run between them is genuinely
// empty -- there is no naturals-shaped question this function cannot express, so it never needs a
// could-not-say arm and a caller reading [] here is reading a computed emptiness. That is the
// property the bucketed forms did not have: they returned [] both for a genuinely empty run and
// for a width they could not spell, which is the empty-observation narrow DESIGN section 5 names.
// Callers that must distinguish "no work" from "cannot express the work" must not get that
// distinction from this function; it has only the first meaning.
// THE ACCUMULATOR IS THE COST SHAPE AND IT IS NOT INCIDENTAL. The naive form,
// concat([lo], nat_range_inclusive(lo + 1, hi)), is QUADRATIC on this runtime: rc_list_concat
// extends its LEFT operand by cloning every element of its RIGHT one, so consing a singleton onto
// the recursive tail copies the whole tail at every step. Accumulating the other way round makes
// the long list the left operand and the singleton the right, which clones one element per step.
// DESIGN section 6 fixes a proven cost-shape defect regardless of the realized n -- n is 7 here
// today, but a run of naturals is substrate and its n is whatever a future caller wants.
fn nat_range_inclusive(lo: Nat, hi: Nat) -> List<Nat> {
  nat_range_accumulate(lo: lo, hi: hi, acc: [])
}

fn nat_range_accumulate(lo: Nat, hi: Nat, acc: List<Nat>) -> List<Nat> {
  if lo > hi {
    acc
  } else {
    nat_range_accumulate(lo: lo + 1, hi: hi, acc: concat(acc, [lo]))
  }
}
